As Peter Snelling puts it in rec.games.hack:
> I really like this idea.  An initially seeded random number would control
> the layout of the dungeon, and maybe even the generation of monsters and 
> items.  It wouldn't control other things (like the initial randomizing 
> of the identities, or the chances of hitting/missing, which would still
> be "random").

> I can hear the discussions now:
>   - You should try playing 62987!  It has the biggest shop I'd ever
>     seen on level 2, and an even bigger one on level 3, and three
>     differently aligned temples on level 3...
>   - Game 2435 has no random alters or shops in the entire game!
>   - Try playing game 837 as a Tourist.  It's *impossible* to even
>     get off level 1!

> To do this, I guess you'd need separate random number functions which
> would be used for things controlled by the initial random seed (which
> would use the standard random number routines if you didn't use a
> seed).

It could be done.  If a good random generator is programmed in, such that
the same series of numbers is generated given the same seed, then stuff
such as...

	dungeon creation
	item generation
	monster generation

would all use this rnd # gen, while monster actions, chance of hitting, 
damage, etc, would all use the system rand().  This would be a start
at insuring that all levels, monsters, and generated items are the same.

Unfortunely, things like wands or scrolls of create monster can screw
this up, especially in the hands of a monster.

Of course, the other option is that ALL levels/initial monsters on levels/
items on levels are generated at the start of the game.  Of course, this
means the generation of 75+ levels (is this right?) as well as the 
storage of these.  My poor old Amiga 500 would have a fit!   However, this
in essence would tend to make games started with the same random seed
be relatively equal.

